ue  uh working on the basis that the united  states of america as a democracy i would  prefer to talk to the voters and  who are whose minds i hope are still  open and available to logical thought  i mean has it worked so david have these  films really  worked to move public opinion  well all i can say is that things  changed and changed radically  uh in the 1950s i think perhaps  people who appeared on television  holding woolly monkeys and things  people thought oh they're like  entertaining and amusing and so on  but i didn't think they were  particularly important  and that has changed radically  and it's difficult to know when it  started i would say perhaps about 10  years ago  perhaps not more than that not much more  than that anyway that  natural history programs after all  natural history programmes for example  they were not shown on television in  many countries  america's idea of natural history  program was big game  and you couldn't persuade a network to  do a program about a wood wasp  you know not for the life of you  now that has changed and changed  worldwide  and and people are now very well  informed about the natural world  um and i'm i'm i'm delighted that that  should be so  people are more informed about the  natural world but what about their  changes in behavior  behavior very far from everyone who  watches will think of going vegan  stopping flying or  divesting from fossil fuels perhaps in  their  pension portfolio and indeed i mean  which of those which of those options  do you act on most personally yes i i  i haven't eaten red meat for several  years  and i as a biologist i i never had any  objection to eating  red meat i decided that my digestion and  my teeth suggested i'm an omnivore and  so  if there's a biological logic uh or  morality  uh it was okay for me to eat meat and  like a lion each  antenope um but i don't feel that now um  and i i just  don't want to eat meat anymore do you  fly less  um yes i suppose i do um  but i i 